/// @page project_templates_page Project Templates
///
/// @anchor project_templates
/// `dekaf2project` creates a new project from a template: the sources, a CMake file
/// that finds the installed dekaf2, and build directories for Release, Debug and, on
/// macOS, Xcode.
///
/// @code{.sh}
/// dekaf2project -type restserver -with db,service MyServer
/// @endcode
///
/// `dekaf2project -help` lists the project types with their features.
///
/// #### Project Types and Features
///
/// A project type is a directory in the templates directory. Its `manifest.xml`
/// describes the type and declares its features, the parts that can be added to a
/// project of this type:
///
/// @code{.xml}
/// <manifest>
/// 	<description>
/// 	a REST server implementation
/// 	</description>
/// 	<feature name="db">pooled database connectors</feature>
/// 	<feature name="service">run as a service of systemd, launchd or the Windows service manager</feature>
/// </manifest>
/// @endcode
///
/// A project gets the base of its type and the features that `-with` names, in a comma
/// separated list. `-with all` adds all features of the type, and `all` is therefore no
/// valid name for a feature.
///
/// #### Files of a Feature
///
/// The files that only a feature needs are in a directory with the name of the feature
/// and a leading `+`. A `+db` directory in the directory of a project type holds files
/// of that type only, and a `+db` directory in the templates directory holds files that
/// all project types with the feature share. The content of both is added to the
/// project, and a file of a feature must not exist in the project already.
///
/// #### Lines of a Feature
///
/// Marker lines select the lines of a feature in any file of a template:
///
/// @code{.cpp}
/// //@if service
/// 	return KService::Run("__LowerProjectName__", argc, argv, RunMain);
/// //@else
/// 	return RunMain(argc, argv);
/// //@endif
/// @endcode
///
/// `//@if !service` selects lines for a project without the feature. In files with `#`
/// comments, like CMake files, the markers start with `#@` instead of `//@`. Markers can
/// be indented and nested, and they are removed from the project. A marker with an
/// unknown feature, or an `if` without `endif`, stops the creation of the project with
/// the name of the file and the line.
///
/// #### Variables
///
/// After the marker lines, variables of the form `__Name__` are replaced, in the
/// contents and in the names of the files:
///
/// | Variable                             | Value                                                |
/// |--------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
/// | `__ProjectName__`                    | the project name                                     |
/// | `__LowerProjectName__`               | the project name in lowercase                        |
/// | `__UpperProjectName__`               | the project name in uppercase                        |
/// | `__ProjectVersion__`                 | the version from `-version`                          |
/// | `__ProjectType__`                    | the project type                                     |
/// | `__ProjectPath__`                    | the path from `-path`                                |
/// | `__SSOProvider__`, `__SSOScope__`    | the URL and scope from `-sso`                        |
/// | `__Dekaf2Version__`                  | the version of dekaf2                                |
/// | `__SourceFiles__`, `__HeaderFiles__` | the .cpp and .h files of the project, for CMake      |
